  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the organization of a respiratory syncytial virus particle and its genome. The main image shows a cutaway of an enveloped, roughly filamentous virion. A surrounding membrane contains several surface proteins: the G and F glycoproteins project outward, while SH and M2-1 are also associated with the envelope region. Just beneath the membrane is the M protein layer, which appears to line and support the viral envelope.\n\nInside the particle, the viral RNA is not free but wrapped by N protein into a helical ribonucleoprotein complex. This long coiled structure fills the interior and is associated with the polymerase components P and L, indicating the machinery used for transcription and replication of the single-stranded RNA genome.\n\nThe lower schematic links this particle structure to gene order along the genome. The genes are arranged from the 3′ end to the 5′ end and include NS1, NS2, N, P, M, SH, G, F, M2, and L. Small marks above M2 indicate two products or reading frames. Overall, the figure conveys how the genome encodes the structural and replication proteins positioned in specific parts of the virion.",
